A||||37||wayne||brakes||||1||
Z||000000||wayne||12-01-1999||12:34 PM||||Has anyone had any expericienc with the brake upgrade by West Coast Fiero?<P>wayne||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||12-01-1999||04:36 PM||||Expect an extremely long wait for your shipment if you order anything from Chris West a.k.a. West Coast Fiero and don't expect him to return many of your emails.||||reg||1
Z||000002||wayne||12-02-1999||07:15 AM||||thanks<P>Does anyone know of any other good brake upgrades.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||12-03-1999||09:44 AM||||I dont know anyting about the West Coast Fiero upgrade, but there is all kinds of info available on the net about the Grand Am upgrade.  You should also do a search on here on the forum for this as well.||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||12-03-1999||10:28 AM||||The Grand Am upgrade looks very appealing, especially if you've got an automatic trans Fiero. Appealing because it appears that it may actually cost less to do this upgrade than the cost of rebuilding/replacing the stock rear brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||12-03-1999||11:16 PM||||First, a couple links to hifi expensive racing style brake upgrades.<P> <A HREF="http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/pisa1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/pisa1.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/brakes.htm[/url" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/brakes.htm</A> <P>Now a URL to an article about the Grand Am brake swap.<P> <A HREF="http://members.tripod.com/Randy_Agee/brakes2.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.tripod.com/Randy_Agee/brakes2.html</A> <P>I've personally done the Grand Am brake swap on my Batmobile, the hardest part is converting the old rotors into hubs. Go to this next link for pics of my completed brake swap.<P> <A HREF="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/brakes" TARGET=_blank>http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/brakes</A><P>I believe West Coast Fiero uses 4X4 S-10 calipers and rotors (10.25" diameter). Since I can't find decent tires for my 14" wheels, I might go to bigger wheels (maybe 16"). If so, then I may try to engineer my own caliper mounting bracket for the S-10 brakes, which will be the hardest part of the swap if you're doing it yourself.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-04-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||12-06-1999||07:03 AM||||A few questions about brake upgrades.<BR>I've got an 85SE 2m6 4spd and want to keep my parking brake functional.  Since the front brakes do most of the work anyway what if I only replaced the fronts with grand am brakes?  Would the proportioning be screwed up?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-06-1999||07:27 AM||||Yes.  At least that's what I read.  (I think in one of batboys' links?).  Great braking on the front and poor on the rear.  I'd hate to get caught in the rain (or worse, the snow) with a set up like that.  Just food for thought.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||12-06-1999||07:38 AM||||On my swap, I did the front brakes first and then tried it for a short while. At slow speeds on gravel, the back brakes would not lock up when you slam on the brakes. I went ahead and did the rear then too, but I had already planned on doing both anyway. NOTE: you want the front to lock up first and then the rear.<P>I've heard of several people that have done just the front swap and then put in an adjustable proportioning valve. You have to play around a bit to get the right setting, but supposedly it works good. As everyone knows, the front brakes do a little more braking than the rear so this could be a viable option.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-06-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||88 Fiero||12-06-1999||06:52 PM||||Chris West's brakes are awsome...it will really snap your neck when you step on those brakes...Another company that you want to call is "RCC Specialty". They uses Corvette rotors and Calipers...They also have the Master Cylinder replacement if you want to keep the stock pedal height.. I forgot the size of the rotors.. They even indicated that you could have either a Fiero name or Pontiac printed on the Caliper...Also a company named "Move-it" also have a brake kits for the '84 to '87 Fiero's. Only the fronts tho'.. and it uses the Porsche cross-drill race package...very mean looking brakes... I've seen some import cars with the Porsche's brakes on it....||||reg||1
Z||000010||DJRice||12-07-1999||09:35 AM||||I havent tried this technique yet, but I see many late model Nissans and Hondas that have coated their Calipers with Hi-Temp Yellow paint.  I believe this improves brake performance and may even offer a HP increase.  <BR>I have heard that Orange or Red can be used as well, but I believe that yellow offers the best performance increase.  <P>Can anyone veryify this?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I wouldnt have posted this if I hadnt seen a Nissan with bright yellow calipers yesterday on the way home from work.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||12-07-1999||09:38 AM||||BTW, no offense to any Fiero owners who have performed this upgrade, but I get a kick out of cars with that as thier <B>only</B> upgrade.  I mean, at least put some stickers on...||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phil||12-07-1999||10:22 AM||||HP increase from PAINT on the Brakes???????||||reg||1
Z||000013||Carrolles||12-07-1999||12:07 PM||||Yes.  And if it the right shade of yellow it makes the base sound as if you have a subwoofer installed.||||reg||1
Z||000014||DaRkLoRD||12-07-1999||05:17 PM||||and we can't forget the huge improvement from a vtec sticker.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>sorry. getting really off-topic here.. hehe..||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||12-07-1999||05:36 PM||||You don't want ANY wheel to lock up.  Batboy is right, though.  Better to have the front lock up than the rear.  A sliding wheel will always lead.  If the front locks up, you can't steer, but at least you'll keep the nose in the right direction (it will go straight as an arrow-bad if you're in a curve!).  If the rear brakes lock up, you get to back up at 65mph!  I'd do both at the same time.  It'll be worth the extra $$ when it starts to rain and some idiot with his head stuck up his a** decides to pull out in front of you.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Standard||12-08-1999||03:11 AM||||most car companies <B>severly</B> reduce the pressure to the back brakes with the proportioning valve. Take a look at <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/4/we.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/4/we.htm</A>  and click on the "high performance brakes" section, proportioning valve info is on page 3, i think.  tons of other info, too.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||GREENDANO||12-09-1999||06:00 PM||||I have/had yellow Grand AM calipers using the VHT caliper paint.  The only improvement by painting them was looks. Huge improvement in braking performance with the GA conversion.  I did my 85 GT a couple years ago and have all the parts ready to install on my F40 kit. <BR>HEY Batboy, I got a V-8 now!!!!  No more V-6 for Dan-O.<BR>  I also chamfered the outside face of the rotors to make it look like they're  crossdrilled.  I don't have pictures but I'll look into borrowing my buddies digtal camera if enough people are interested.  <BR>  Dan-O<p>[This message has been edited by GREENDANO (edited 12-09-1999).]||||reg||4
Z||000018||batboy||12-09-1999||09:10 PM||||Dan-o!!! I wondered what happened to you, buddy. I heard you're working on a kit car. Seems like I'm getting more and more competition with V8's, but that cool.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Phil||12-09-1999||10:22 PM||||V8s- how crude||||reg||6
Z||000020||Patrick||12-09-1999||11:28 PM||||<BR>Yeah, how crude...<P>... now if I just had the time and patience to take the hi-performance 350 out of my rusty Z28 and drop it into my '87 Coupe...<P>Nah, much too crude. &lt;sigh&gt;||||reg||14
Z||000021||GREENDANO||12-10-1999||09:33 PM||||Crude?  How's that?<BR>  I got the car with the V8 installed already and body partially completed.  The whole car is rough but fixable.<BR>  Batboy, where d'ya hear about my kit acquisition?  That's cool that you found out, I didn't know I was that popular.   <BR>  I have machined a few hubs for people in the past and can do a couple more if the need arises.  E-mail me privately if you're interested.  <BR>  After reading my first post, I realized my note was confusing.  What I meant was that I cut a patern in the rotor face with a chamfering bit only on the surface so the rotors look like they are crossed drilled, but their integrity has not been compromised.<BR>  Dan-O<p>[This message has been edited by GREENDANO (edited 12-10-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000022||batboy||12-11-1999||12:08 AM||||Dan-o, don't worry about Phil or Patrick, they're both good Fiero buddies and are just teasing us. About finding out about your project, I have my spies! For any of you other Forum members that are serious about trying this Grand Am swap, Dan-o is the man to help you with the front hubs. He did mine for me and they turned out great. Once the old front rotors have been turned down into hubs, the rest is easy.||||reg||1
Z||000023||william_thorniii||12-11-1999||09:25 PM||||Hey, I have also heard that F-body rotors with Corvette calipers is an AWESOME upgrade.  I think the F-body calipers are 11.25".  You need to upgrade your wheels with this one too, but I am planning on doing that anyway.<P>About proportioning... front brakes usually do about 80% of the braking as a rough guestimate.  You understand this well if you have ever tried to stop an old Buick... your forehead just about hits the dash from the dive.  <P>About cross drilling... not a good idea for every day drivers.  If you want to know more, e-mail me personally... I might be able to dig out my notes.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000024||Patrick||12-11-1999||09:59 PM||||<BR>Dan-O, batboy's right. We're just teasing you V8 guys. I can't speak for Phil, but I'm simply envious of anyone who has a V8 in their Fiero. Must be the result of having owned hi-performance Chevy's for years...<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000025||GREENDANO||12-12-1999||09:22 AM||||William,<BR>  I couldn't agree with you more about cross drilling.  If you look at a previous post you'll see it was me who was leading the crusade for not wasting money on crossdrilled rotors.  I only surface drilled the face of the rotor like maybe 1/8"  It only made a little divot in the face.  I have not effected the integrity of the rotor.  Cross drilling a set of over the counter rotors in DANGEROUS!!!   Those rotors were not designed to have holes in them.  The marks I made are less than the depth of a groove you get by using really hard $6.00 lifetime warranty brake pads.  <BR>  The end result is a rotor that looks crossdrilled, but cost $13.  BTW, I'm going for the high performance look because they're going on an F40 kit.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||10
Z||000026||Phil||12-12-1999||10:20 AM||||V8s how crude heheheheh||||reg||1
Z||000027||Raydar||12-13-1999||10:54 PM||||Okay... sounds like some real brake knowledge goin' on here. Hope somebody can help. I just installed the CarboMet brake pads from the Fiero Store, 4 new rotors, and rebuilt rear calipers (88). Have to really stand on the brakes to stop. Locking them up is probably out of the question. Surely this isn't normal, is it? Do these pads have to be really hot before they start to grip? (Seems like I read that about metallic linings, several years back.)<BR>Any advice will be greatly appreciated. ||||reg||1
Z||000028||Standard||12-14-1999||01:33 AM||||Most brake pads need a break-in period.  Right after you install them you should lightly apply the brakes from around 20mph to a complete stop a few times to let them get warm, and be easy on the brakes for the first few drives.  unless I'm thinking about new rotors.. in that case just ignore me.||||reg||1
Z||000029||geyser||12-14-1999||01:58 AM||||If those new pads are semi metallic you'll need to perform a half dozen good panic stops, hitting the brakes hard from about 35-40 MPH. Find an open parking lot or clear street with no one or no thing around and make your panic stops- you want to bring the braking to just before lockup. Without this process you new pads will not have a proper break-in.||||reg||1
Z||000030||batboy||12-14-1999||07:33 AM||||You may not of got the the brakes bled enough (might still be air in the system). Also, it does take a few stops for the calipers to pump back up, because you have to compress the disc pads back to install them. I don't know about doing a bunch of panic stops. I've heard you should do a few easy 20 MPH stops, let the brakes cool and then try it again.||||reg||1
Z||000031||RFMike||12-14-1999||08:08 AM||||Standard,<BR>I finally got around to looking at the page you posted.  That is a great post!  I had never considered the brake upgrade because I didn't want to do the rear and lose my parking brake.  This article gives a low cost fix if you upgrade the front brakes only, by changing out the spring in the proportioning valve.  Probably cost him ten bucks to by the series of springs he experimented with.  I'm going to upgrade the front brakes and tune the p-valve when it gets warm (I hate working in the cold- bad circulation in my hands).<BR>Standard, you win the cupie doll!<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000032||RFMike||12-14-1999||08:21 AM||||I was so impressed with this guys solution, I'm going to post a portion of it.  I strongly recommend reading the whole thing- this guy is good.<P>      "My favorite freeway exit ramp is a 270 degree turn that I can usually take at about 50 MPH. To get a baseline on my car, I took this turn and right in the middle of it, I slammed on the brakes and noted the car's reaction. As expected, the front wheels locked up and I began to plow through the turn. I let up on the brakes to straighten it up and continued through the turn. Then I performed this test two more times just to make sure that I did experience what I thought I did. And to reduce the chance that there was something unusual about this part of<BR>the turn, I varied the point where I did the braking.<P>      Now how am I going to determine the amount of pressure reduction the valve is causing? I suppose if I knew the spring constant, I could<BR>calculate the pressure based on the diameter of the spool and a guess of the amount of its deflection. Even if I did that, it would still be a guess. Instead, I removed the spring from the valve and measured the major diameter as .53", the wire diameter as .063", and the free length as 1.3". I crossed it with a spring in the Lee Spring Catalog, LC-063GH6, which has a diameter of .54", a wire cross section of .063" and a free length of 1.25". The rate for this spring is 35 pounds per inch, which is closer than any calculation I could make with all my guessing. <P>      Again I thought "How much should I change it?" Who knows. I bought several springs that had the same O.D. and free length, but had wire diameters of .058" through .041". This would give me spring rates of 26 pounds per inch through 8 pounds per inch, respectively. I then replaced the original spring with the .058" spring and ran my exit ramp test. Oh, by the way, did I mention my disclaimer? I continued to reduce the wire diameter until I got the rear wheels to lock up just after the fronts locked. This is how I want my brakes to function.<P>Results<BR>      I could use a one word description like "oustanding" or "awesome" to describe the improvement of the brakes, but I wanted to know numbers. I thought about buying one of those G-Tech instruments, but I didn't think I'd use it for much else. Besides, I'm not sure how accurate it could be dangling from the windshield of the car. I figured that a stopwatch would be just about as accurate, so that's how I tested the new brakes.<P>      Back on the freeway, I made some panic stops and measured the time it took the car to stop. I started the stop watch as I pressed the pedal, then I stopped the stopwatch just as the car came to a halt. I know there are better ways to get more accurate information, but since there are no test tracks near me, this method was good enough to get a ball park reading of the stopping distance.<P>      The median stopping time for 5 tests was 3.0 seconds. I used these equations for physical motion to determine the stopping distance:<BR>                                                a = V - Vo                  <BR>                                                     t             <BR>      and<P>                                              S = Vot + 1/2at2                  <P>      where<P>a     is the acceleration in feet/second2 (assumed constant)<BR>V     is the final velocity (0 mph)<BR>Vo    is the initial velocity in feet/sec (60 mph = 88 f/s)<BR>t     is the braking time in seconds, and<BR>S     is the braking distance in feet"<P>He came up with an estimated 132 feet for his stopping distance.  By his method, this is an estimate.  Bottom line is, with the front upgrade, and taking better advantage of the rear brakes' capacity, this should give mach better breaking.  Can you tell I'm excited?<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000033||Raydar||12-14-1999||10:23 AM||||Thanks to all who responded to my post. <BR>Mike... Interesting way to test. Would have liked to have seen the looks on the faces of the drivers following him.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000034||Monkeyman||12-14-1999||11:27 AM||||RFMike--Granted I'm not the most technical guy in the world, but...WHAT?!??  ||||reg||5
Z||000035||RFMike||12-14-1999||11:04 PM||||I didn't mean to overwhelm anybody with equations.  The point I was trying to reinforce was that the stock rear brakes have the same stopping capacity as the stock front brakes, but the actual capacity of the rear brakes is reduced by the P-valve.  If you upgrade the front, you can have balanced braking by tweaking the P-valve, without upgrading the rear.<BR>The End.<BR>BTW, all the equations the author used are covered in entry level (first quarter college or high school college prep) physics class.  Probably not that interesting; I won't do it again.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Monkeyman||12-14-1999||11:30 PM||||Sorry.  I went to college for a while (OK, only a year), but never studied physics.  They never taught us any of that in psychology class. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000037||Standard||12-15-1999||03:52 AM||||Where's my cupie doll!  I'd prefer a 1988 Fiero GT, ya know...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  donations are accepted!  <P>That website had lots of great stuff!  Dig through the archives!||||reg||14
